==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/twɪt͡ʃ/|[tʰw̥ɪt͡ʃ]}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-twitch.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪtʃ|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|twicchen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*twiċċan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*twikkijan||to nail, pin, fasten, clasp, pinch}}.

Cognate with {{cog|en|tweak}}, {{cog|nds|twikken}}, {{cog|nds-de|twicken||to pinch, pinch off}}, {{m|goh|zweckōn}} and {{m|goh|gizwickan}} (> German {{m|de|zwicken||to pinch}}).

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A brief, small (sometimes involuntary) movement out of place and then back again; a [[spasm]].
#: {{syn|en|tic}}
#: {{ux|en|I saw a little '''twitch''' in the man's face, and knew he was lying.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} Action of spotting or seeking out a bird, especially a rare one.
# {{lb|en|farriery}} A [[stick]] with a [[hole]] in one end through which passes a [[loop]], which can be drawn tightly over the upper [[lip]] or an [[ear]] of a [[horse]] and [[twist]]ed to keep the animal quiet during minor [[surgery]].
#: {{syn|en|barnacle}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1861|author=w:John Henry Walsh|title=The Horse in the Stable and in the Field
|passage=THE '''TWITCH''' is a short stick of strong ash, about the size of a mopstick, with a hole pierced near the end, through which is passed a piece of strong but small cord, and tied in a loop large enough to admit the open hand freely.}}
# {{lb|en|physiology}} A brief, contractile response of a skeletal muscle elicited by a single maximal volley of impulses in the neurons supplying it.
# {{lb|en|mining}} The sudden narrowing almost to nothing of a [[vein]] of [[ore]].
# {{lb|en|birdwatching}} A trip taken in order to observe a rare bird.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1982|author=|title=|journal={{w|British Birds (magazine)|British Birds}}|location=London|volume=75|issue=11|page=498|column=|text=Siberian Crane [...] Following its departure, there was much speculation that rediscovery at a less inaccessible locality would lead to one of the great '''twitches''' of 1981; alas, this was not to be.}}

